{
  "title": "Food and drink",
  "title_target": "Bia agus deoch",
  "goal": "Order food and drink, name dishes, and talk about taste.",
  "bekal": [
    "order in a café or restaurant with *Ba mhaith liom …*",
    "name foods, drinks, and mealtimes",
    "describe taste with *blasta, milis, goirt, te*"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Comhrá — Sa bhialann",
      "intro": "Budi and Seán have lunch in a small restaurant in the city center. The waiter greets them in Irish.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Freastalaí:",
          "text": "Dia daoibh. An bhfuil sibh réidh le hordú?",
          "id": "Hello. Are you ready to order?",
          "literal": "God to-you-all. question are you-all ready for ordering?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Seán:",
          "text": "Tá. Ba mhaith liom anraith agus ceapaire cáise.",
          "id": "Yes. I'd like soup and a cheese sandwich.",
          "literal": "am. would good with-me soup and sandwich cheese-of."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Cad a mholann tú?",
          "id": "What do you recommend?",
          "literal": "what that recommends you?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Freastalaí:",
          "text": "Tá an bradán an-mhaith inniu. Nó an stobhach — tá sé traidisiúnta.",
          "id": "The salmon is very good today. Or the stew — it's traditional.",
          "literal": "is the salmon very-good today. or the stew — is it traditional."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Ba mhaith liom an stobhach, le do thoil. Tá ocras mór orm!",
          "id": "I'll have the stew, please. I'm starving!",
          "literal": "would good with-me the stew, with your will. is hunger big on-me!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Freastalaí:",
          "text": "Go breá. Agus le hól?",
          "id": "Fine. And to drink?",
          "literal": "fine. and for drinking?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Gloine uisce, le do thoil. Tá tart orm freisin.",
          "id": "A glass of water, please. I'm thirsty too.",
          "literal": "glass water, with your will. is thirst on-me too."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Seán:",
          "text": "Agus pionta bainne domsa!",
          "id": "And a pint of milk for me!",
          "literal": "and pint milk for-me!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Freastalaí:",
          "text": "Ceart go leor. Beidh sé réidh i gceann deich nóiméad.",
          "id": "All right. It'll be ready in ten minutes.",
          "literal": "right enough. will-be it ready in head ten minutes."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Tá an stobhach seo an-bhlasta. Tá sé beagán milis.",
          "id": "This stew is delicious. It's a bit sweet.",
          "literal": "is the stew this very-tasty. is it a-bit sweet."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Seán:",
          "text": "Sea, tá prátaí agus cairéid ann. An bhfuil tú sásta?",
          "id": "Yes, it has potatoes and carrots in it. Are you happy with it?",
          "literal": "is so, are potatoes and carrots in-it. question are you satisfied?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Tá mé an-sásta. An bille, le do thoil!",
          "id": "Very happy. The bill, please!",
          "literal": "am I very-satisfied. the bill, with your will!"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**Ceapaire cáise — the ingredient goes into the possessive form.** \"Cheese sandwich\" comes out as \"sandwich of-cheese\": *cáis* becomes *cáise*. Same pattern for *cupán tae* (a cup of tea), *gloine uisce* (a glass of water), *pionta bainne* (a pint of milk). For now, learn the pairs; forming the possessive yourself is A2 material.",
        "**Le hól, le hordú — the h slips in again.** The preposition *le* adds an *h* before a following word that starts with a vowel: *le hól* (\"to drink\"), *le hordú* (\"to order\"), *le hithe* (\"to eat\"). You've now seen this *h* pattern after *go*, *na*, *cé*, *ní*, numbers, and *le* — six places, one habit.",
        "**Beidh sé réidh — \"it will be ready\".** *Beidh* is the future form of *tá*, and it behaves exactly the same: *beidh mé*, *beidh tú*, *beidh sé*. The full future tense comes in Unit 18; for now just recognize *beidh* when you hear it, and use it to promise something.",
        "**Domsa, liomsa — emphatic forms.** Adding *-sa* or *-se* to a prepositional pronoun gives it stress, like saying \"for **me**\" with your voice raised. *Dom* → *domsa*, *liom* → *liomsa*, *agam* → *agamsa*, *uirthi* → *uirthise*. Use them when there's a contrast: *Tae domsa, caife duitse*."
      ]
    }
  ],
